<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Locke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1632">1632</a>–<a href="/years/1704">1704</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Locke was an English philosopher whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/two-treatises-of-government">Two Treatises of Government</a> and Essay Concerning Human Understanding, published around 1689, shaped liberal political thought and empiricism. As secretary to Anthony Ashley Cooper he helped draft the Fundamental Constitutions of Carolina. He lived in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> in the 1680s and argued that government rests on the consent of the governed and must protect life, liberty and property.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-locke.jpg" alt="John Locke" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Godfrey Kneller · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godfrey_Kneller_-_Portrait_of_John_Locke_(Hermitage).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Relative freedom of the press made Leiden and Amsterdam centres of European publishing, where René Descartes, Baruch Spinoza and <b>John Locke</b> lived and wrote, while Christiaan Huygens, Antonie van Leeuwenhoek and the Leiden medical school of Herman Boerhaave advanced science.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the seventeenth century England became a centre of scientific inquiry: the Royal Society was founded in 1660, Isaac Newton published his Principia in 1687, and Thomas Hobbes and <b>John Locke</b> laid foundations of modern political thought.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Locke</b>, back from exile in the Dutch Republic, published in 1689 (though their title pages bear the date 1690) An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, which argued that the mind begins as “white paper” furnished by experience, and Two Treatises of Government, which grounded government in the consent…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>…of limited government; persecuted groups such as the Huguenots and the English Puritans carried their faith abroad, the Puritans to New England from the 1620s; and the long experience of religious war strengthened the case for toleration that <b>John Locke</b> and others made later in the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Locke</b>’s Two Treatises of Government (1689) declared that absolute monarchy “is indeed inconsistent with civil society, and so can be no form of civil-government at all”, since subjects had no common judge to appeal to between themselves and their ruler.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Locke</b>’s Two Treatises of Government, published in 1689 though largely written earlier, grounded government in the consent of the governed and made the legislature the supreme power of the commonwealth.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wrington, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in High Laver, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.36,-2.76&amp;year=1678" aria-label="Wrington on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4487 385 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John Locke"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4937 655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4937 655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.36,-2.76&amp;year=1678">What was here in 1678? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Where-ever law ends, tyranny begins, if the law be transgressed to another’s harm…&#8221;</p><footer>Second Treatise of Government, section 202 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7370/pg7370-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let us then suppose the mind to be, as we say, white paper, void of all characters, without any ideas:—How comes it to be furnished?&#8221;</p><footer>An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, book 2, chapter 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/10615/pg10615-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;A sound mind in a sound body, is a short, but full description of a happy state in this world. He that has these two, has little more to wish for…&#8221;</p><footer>Some Thoughts Concerning Education, section 1 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Some_Thoughts_Concerning_Education/Part_I" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Though the earth, and all inferior creatures, be common to all men, yet every man has a property in his own person: this no body has any right to but himself.&#8221;</p><footer>Second Treatise of Government, section 27 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7370/pg7370-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the end of law is not to abolish or restrain, but to preserve and enlarge freedom: for in all the states of created beings capable of laws, where there is no law, there is no freedom…&#8221;</p><footer>Second Treatise of Government, section 57 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7370/pg7370-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thus in the beginning all the world was America, and more so than that is now; for no such thing as money was any where known.&#8221;</p><footer>Second Treatise of Government, section 49 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/7370/pg7370-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
